{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188353": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Law, Tenure Track, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-29 Location: Orange The Fowler School of Law at Chapman University invites applications for one or more tenure-track faculty positions, to commence in August 2027.  The appointment will be made at either the Assistant or Associate Professor rank, commensurate with the candidate’s qualifications and experience. Chapman University is a nationally ranked, R2 Carnegie Classified, private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing 50 states, three territories, and 78 countries. The 11 schools and colleges of Chapman, including the Fowler School of Law, offer 65 bachelors, 53 masters, and six doctoral programs, along with nearly 60 minors and 17 accelerated and bridge programs. Classes are taught by the more than 1,100 dedicated faculty members who are leaders in their fields, including a Nobel Prize recipient, MacArthur Fellows, National Medal of Science honorees, and Emmy, Grammy, and Academy Award winners. Chapman University's Dale E. Fowler School of Law is an ABA-accredited, private law school located on the main campus in Orange, California, 35 miles south of Los Angeles. The law school's high-caliber faculty includes three former U.S. Supreme Court clerks, a Nobel Prize winner, a former five-term U.S. congressman, and a former general counsel for a major Hollywood studio.   The Fowler School of Law emphasizes personalized legal education. We are a strong academic community with connections to local, regional, national, and international lawyers' associations, a very high bar passage rate, a great record of students' employment after graduation, a low student-to-faculty ratio, curricular offerings with breadth and depth across multiple areas of specialty, and interscholastic moot court, mediation, and alternative dispute resolution teams ranked among the top of national law schools.  Students especially value close connection with their professors, a vibrant and engaged co-curricular environment, and a technologically modern facility (including an electronically cutting-edge trial courtroom) located in easy walking distance to the coffee shops, restaurants, and cultural attractions of Old Towne Orange and Orange County, California. Responsibilities: Engage in scholarly research, leading to publications in high-quality journals. Teach a range of courses that reflect the specialty interests of the candidate and the needs of the law school. Actively contribute as an integral member of the Fowler School of Law faculty by engaging in department and university service as expected of an Assistant or Associate Professor. Plan, develop, and deliver high-quality courses. Offer regular office hours to provide students with opportunities for one-on-one consultations, academic advising, and clarification of course materials. Contribute to the overall mission of the institution by attending faculty and student meetings and supporting campus-wide initiatives and events. Serve on academic committees and task forces within the institution, contributing to discussions and decisions related to academic policies, curriculum development, and trends in the law. Tenure-track faculty members are evaluated on their scholarship, teaching, and service. Qualified candidates in any area of academic specialization will be considered. Candidates with research and teaching interests in Torts, Civil Procedure, Criminal Law, Constitutional Law, Employment Law, and Property are especially welcome — but candidates outside those fields are very much encouraged as well.  Entry-level candidates should demonstrate potential for strong teaching and scholarship. Candidates must have outstanding academic credentials, including: J.D. from an accredited law school or an equivalent degree (such as a Ph.D. in a related field). A record of scholarly achievement or the potential thereof. While previous publications in academic journals are not required, the candidate must have high desire and likelihood to be a productive scholar. A record of effective teaching or the potential thereof. For appointment as Associate, a candidate should have published at least two academic articles of high merit in a law journal or equivalent outlet and demonstrated experience as a teacher, though these are not absolute requirements and equivalent experience can be considered. Qualified candidates are invited to submit the following electronically: Letter of application Curriculum Vitae Reprints of publications or other examples of scholarly work (no more than three) Representative sampling of teaching evaluations or statement of teaching philosophy/experience for those without teaching experience A list of 3 professional or academic references (references will not be contacted until a candidate is a finalist for the position and the candidate is notified in advance). Inquiries can be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Professor Tom Campbell at tcampbell@chapman.edu .  Please use \"Tenure-Track Faculty Vacancy\" as the email subject line.  All inquiries and applications are confidential as allowed by state and federal law. Application reviews will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled.  Salary is $140,000-$165,000 and will be commensurate with appointment rank, experience, and internal equity.  This position is contingent on final budget approval.  Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate.",
      "department": "Fowler School of Law"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190412": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer Pool, School of Communication, 2026-2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01 Location: Orange Campus General Information The School of Communication at Chapman University seeks applications for its Part-Time Lecturer Pool for potential teaching opportunities in the following areas/disciplines: Communication Studies Strategic Communication Appointments from the part-time pool are temporary and often made just prior to the start of the academic term, so applicants must be available at times on very short notice. The Fall term begins mid-August and ends late-December; the Spring term begins mid-January and ends mid-May. Chapman University is a nationally-ranked institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. The University achieved R2 status in the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education, a distinction held by just 10 percent of all U.S. universities and is ranked as #110 among Best National Universities by US News and World Report (2026). Our faculty include academic leaders who excel in research, publishing, and world-class teaching in our 11 schools and colleges, including the School of Communication . Dedicated to forward-looking, personalized education, we create an environment for unlimited achievement by both our students and faculty. More information about Chapman University is available at www.chapman.edu . The mission of the School of Communication is to educate and prepare students to obtain and evaluate information critically; to create, implement, and disseminate evidence-based messages; and to communicate ethically and appropriately with a variety of audiences in both personal and professional contexts. We aim to produce communication leaders who are aware of the importance of communication research, theories, and principles, and who are able to apply these to communicate effectively in an increasingly complex global communication environment. The School of Communication has experienced a tremendous amount of growth in the past few years. Home to three undergraduate academic programs , a Master of Science in Strategic Communication , and a Ph.D. in Communication , the School of Communication continues to be considered a vibrant and innovative leader in the rapidly growing and critically important field of communication. Required -University teaching experience or related field experience Required -Ability to teach in-person on campus Preferred -Earned MA, MS, or Doctoral degree from an accredited institution in the subject area related to the position Preferred -Professional experience in media, public relations, or strategic communication Qualified candidates are invited to electronically submit the following through Interfolio: Cover letter detailing qualifications related to the position description Curriculum Vitae Names of three references with contact information For any inquiries, please contact Jackie Coyne at cardwell@chapman.edu . Please use Part-Time Lecturer Pool Inquiry as the subject line. The School of Communication will draw from the pool as necessary when openings arise. Only those selected for an interview will be contacted. Applications will remain in the applicant pool until the end of Chapman University's fiscal year, May 31st. Should you remain interested in future openings beyond May 31st, you will need to reapply. The pay range for course and course related work for this position is $46.85 per hour. This position is contingent on final budget approval. Successful completion of a background check is required for all selected candidates.",
      "department": "School of Communication"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189043": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology, Tenure-Track, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-10 Location: Orange, CA General Information The Department of Psychology in the Crean College of Health and Behavioral Sciences at Chapman University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Psychology beginning August 2027. This position is closely linked to our newly established Bachelor of Science (BS) in Psychology, a program designed to prepare students for advanced study in graduate programs and careers the health professions. The BS curriculum emphasizes research methods, statistics, psychobiology, and health science, and this faculty member will play a central role in shaping its identity and trajectory. This is a tenure-track position with 50% of faculty time devoted to research, reflecting Chapman’s commitment to supporting a community of teacher-scholars. Current areas of research expertise in the department include health psychology, cognitive neuroscience, computational psychology, affective science, and developmental science. We welcome faculty whose area of expertise adds to or complements current areas of strength in our faculty. Chapman University is a nationally ranked, R2 Carnegie Classified, private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing 50 states, 4 territories, and 76 countries. The 11 schools and colleges of Chapman, including the Crean College of Health and Behavioral Sciences, offer 50 Bachelors, 29 Masters, and 8 doctoral programs, along with 73 minors and 19 accelerated and bridge programs. Dedicated to forward-looking, personalized education, Chapman offers small class sizes, faculty-mentored student research, and an environment for achievement by both students and faculty. The following links provide additional information about Chapman University’s Crean College of Health and Behavioral Sciences and the Department of Psychology . Responsibilities Develop and maintain a rigorous and productive scholarly program with strong potential for extramural funding Teach undergraduate courses in areas of expertise and develop new courses aligned with departmental needs Advise and mentor undergraduate students Contribute to unit, college, and university service, including the ongoing development of the BS in Psychology program Tenure-track faculty are expected to develop and maintain an active research agenda for future tenure consideration and are evaluated on their research/scholarly activity, teaching and service. We are seeking a scholar who brings rigorous scientific foundations to the study of psychological health and its real-world applications. Example topics include, but are not limited to, digital and AI-assisted approaches to psychological care; evidence-based strategies for treatment personalization; and transdiagnostic approaches to psychopathology. We welcome all strong candidates from across the psychological sciences and are especially interested in those with expertise in clinical/counseling psychology or a research focus with direct clinical applications. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Psychology or a closely related field. A record of, or clear potential for, high-level scholarly activity. Qualified candidates should electronically submit the following materials: Letter of application Curriculum Vitae Statements of research Statement of teaching philosophy Course evaluations from the two most recent years of teaching (if the candidate has prior teaching experience) Three publications or pre-prints in PDF format that best align with the statement of research. Three confidential letters of recommendation Inquiries can be directed to the Search Committee chair Dr. Vincent Berardi at berardi@chapman.edu. Initial application review will begin on October 12, 2026 and will be ongoing until the position is filled. The position will begin in Fall 2027. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate. The salary range for this position is $100,000 to $115,000, with the highest salary reserved for the most experienced candidate. Start-up packages are competitive and will be based on experience, rank, and funding status. Teaching load is consistent with R2 status and will accommodate 50% time devoted to research activities. This position is contingent on final budget approval.",
      "department": "Psychology"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188680": {
      "title": "Clinical Professor of Physician Assistant Studies and Department Chair, Non-Tenure Track, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-14 Location: Irvine, CA Crean College of Health and Behavioral Sciences at Chapman University invites applications for a non-tenure track appointment as Program Director/Chair in Physician Assistant Studies, beginning August 1, 2027. Chapman University is a nationally ranked, R2 Carnegie Classified, private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing fifty states, three territories, and eighty-seven countries. The eleven schools and colleges of Chapman, including Crean College of Health and Behavioral Sciences, offer sixty-five bachelors, fifty-three masters, and six doctoral programs, along with nearly sixty minors and seventeen accelerated and bridge programs. Classes are taught by the more than 1,100 dedicated faculty members who are leaders in their fields, including Nobel Prize recipients, MacArthur Fellows, National Medal of Science honorees, former U.S. Supreme Court clerks, and Emmy, Grammy, and Academy Award winners. The Department of Physician Assistant Studies is located on the Rinker Health Science campus in Irvine, CA. The campus is designed to foster an inter-professional educational environment and to use innovative technology in teaching and practice. The campus features state of the art facilities, including a human cadaver anatomy laboratory, advanced learning center with innovative technology, Grand Rounds auditorium with 3D projection capabilities, multi-sized classrooms, a medical library, and collaborative work centers. The campus is home to several accredited graduate health science professional programs, including Doctor of Physical Therapy, Master of Science in Communication Sciences and Disorders, Master of Science Physician Assistant Studies, and Doctor of Pharmacy. The Rinker campus is strategically located near healthcare institutions, medical device and biopharmaceutical companies. The PA Studies Program has achieved Accreditation-Continued from the ARC-PA through 2030. US News and World Report ranked the PA program 58 th in the nation in 2025. The first cohort of PAs graduated in 2018, and the program graduates 50 PA students each year. The Accreditation Review Commission on Education for the Physician Assistant, Inc. (ARC-PA) has granted Accreditation -Continued status to the Chapman University-Crean College MMS PA Studies Program sponsored by Chapman University. Accreditation-Continued is an accreditation status granted when a currently accredited program is in compliance with the ARC-PA Standards. Accreditation remains in effect until the program closes or withdraws from the accreditation process or until accreditation is withdrawn for failure to comply with the Standards. The approximate date for the next validation review of the program by the ARC-PA will be 2030. The review date is contingent upon continued compliance with the Accreditation Standards and ARC-PA policy. The program’s accreditation history can be viewed on the ARC-PA website at http://www.arc-pa.org/accreditation-history-chapman-university. Program Director/Chair Major Responsibilities Leads the strategic development and direction of the PA program. Communicates program outcomes, opportunities, aspirations, and needs to the college administration, university leadership, and external community. Oversees all aspects of accreditation reporting, re-accreditation planning, self-study, and site-visit. Fosters curriculum innovation and assessment. Maintains stellar student outcomes Evaluates, develops, hires, and retains faculty and staff Manages the program budget Required Qualifications Doctoral-level degree preferred, with PA-C, with at least two years of leadership and administrative experience in PA education. Background appropriate for an appointment as a non-tenure track Clinical Professor. Teaching experience in PA education. Evidence of dynamic leadership embracing data-driven decision making. Embraces collaborative work with the college’s chairs, associate deans, and the dean’s office staff. Preferred Qualifications Ability to incorporate technology with instruction is strongly preferred. Preference will be given to candidates who hold doctoral or other graduate degrees that complement the PA Studies Program curriculum. Awareness of current trends in PA education, including the ethical use of AI in the didactic and clinical portions of the curriculum. Interested and qualified applicants should submit: Letter of application Curriculum Vitae Statement of leadership Statement of teaching philosophy Three confidential letters of recommendation For inquiries about this position, please contact Naveen Jonathan, PA Search Committee Chair, at jonathan@chapman.edu . We offer competitive salaries and excellent benefits that include a substantial contribution toward the cost of medical, vision, and dental insurance, retirement and pension plans, and flexible work arrangements to support work/life balance for our employees. Application review will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate. The salary range for this position is $200,000 to $235,000, with the highest salary reserved for the most experienced candidate. This position is contingent on final budget approval.",
      "department": "Physician Assistant Studies"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189838": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Political Science, Tenure-Track, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17 Location: Orange, CA The Department of Political Science in Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at Chapman University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Political Science with a primary focus on American politics. Successful candidates will also demonstrate the ability to teach courses on research methods. Employment will begin in August 2027. Chapman University is a nationally-ranked, R2 Carnegie Classified, private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing 50 states, three territories, and 87 countries. The 11 schools and colleges of Chapman offer 65 bachelor's, 53 master's, and six doctoral programs, along with nearly 60 minors and 17 accelerated and bridge programs. Classes are taught by the more than 1,100 dedicated faculty members who are leaders in their fields, including Nobel Prize recipients, MacArthur Fellows, National Medal of Science honorees, former U.S. Supreme Court clerks, and Emmy, Grammy, and Academy Award winners. The Department of Political Science is committed to educating students to become effective democratic citizens, both locally and globally. We provide a distinctive, challenging, and personalized learning environment anchored in critical thinking and student research. Through a variety of courses, internships, and other experiential learning opportunities, students gain a theoretical and practical knowledge of politics. Students who graduate with a degree in Political Science will be well-equipped for graduate studies or careers in law, government, public policy, government relations, or the nonprofit sector. The mission of Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences is to foster rigorous inquiry among students through a curriculum grounded in the liberal arts that is scholarly, creative, interdisciplinary, and experiential. The College plays an important and unique role in the educational journey of every Chapman student and aims to ensure that graduates are prepared to assume their responsibility as global citizens, acting knowledgeably and providing compassionate leadership in our community and world. More information about Wilkinson College is available at www.chapman.edu/wilkinson . Responsibilities Plan, develop, and deliver high-quality courses in American politics, as well as in research methods. Maintain an active research agenda in the field of American politics that results in impactful scholarly contributions to the field. Actively participate in departmental, college, and university service. Required A Ph.D. in Political Science or related field from an accredited institution of higher education by August 2027. In-depth knowledge and expertise in the subject area or discipline related to the courses they will be teaching. Record of impactful scholarly publication and/or clear and strong potential for sustained and impactful research productivity. Preferred Experience teaching courses in American politics and/or research methods Evidence of excellence in teaching and a student-centered approach to teaching, including the ability to provide support and guidance to students Experience collaborating with colleagues, department chairs, and academic administrators on curriculum development and institutional initiatives Qualified candidates are invited to submit the following electronically: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Research Statement (2 pages max) Teaching Statement (2 pages max) Three confidential letters of reference Inquiries can be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. John Compton (compton@chapman.edu), and should use ‘American Politics Search’ as the email subject line. Application reviews will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Salary is competitive and will be commensurate with experience. The salary range for this position is $90,000 to $107,000. This position is contingent on final budget approval. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate.",
      "department": "Political Science"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189437": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Philosophy, Tenure Track, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20 Location: Orange, CA Chapman University invites applications for two tenure-track positions at the rank of Assistant Professor in Philosophy, beginning August 1, 2027.  The appointments will be in the Smith Institute for Political Economy and Philosophy and the Philosophy Department within Schmid College of Science and Technology. Successful candidates are expected to conduct independent and original research at the highest academic level, provide excellent teaching, and demonstrate academic leadership. Since the positions are jointly appointed in the Smith Institute, we seek candidates interested in working together with people from different disciplinary backgrounds. Chapman University is a nationally ranked, R2 Carnegie Classified, private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing 50 states, three territories, and 87 countries. The 11 schools and colleges of Chapman, including Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences, offer 65 bachelors, 53 masters, and six doctoral programs, along with nearly 60 minors and 17 accelerated and bridge programs. Classes are taught by the more than 1,100 dedicated faculty members who are leaders in their fields, including Nobel Prize recipients, MacArthur Fellows, National Medal of Science honorees, former U.S. Supreme Court clerks, and Emmy, Grammy, and Academy Award winners. The mission of the Smith Institute is to reintegrate the study of the humanities and economics in the spirit of Adam Smith, the author of The Theory of Moral Sentiments and An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations , and to recombine research and undergraduate education as a discovery process in the spirit of Vernon Smith, a founder of experimental economics. Responsibilities: Conduct independent and original research at the highest academic level, publishing books and/or articles in peer-reviewed journals, depending on the candidate’s specialty areas, and extramural grant-seeking. Teach undergraduate courses and mentor students in Chapman University's Humanomics Program and Philosophy Department. Initial teaching load is 2/2 for a total of 12 credit hours annually. Actively participate in Smith Institute and University service, and community-engagement initiatives. Tenure-track faculty are expected to develop and maintain an active research agenda for future tenure consideration and are evaluated on their research/scholarly activity, teaching and service. Of particular interest will be candidates specialized in one or more of the following: Political Philosophy, Philosophy, Politics and Economics, and the history of political and economic thought. Required: PhD in Philosophy or its equivalent from an accredited institution. Strong potential for impactful scholarly contributions in the field of Philosophy, or a closely related field. Commitment to teaching and mentoring. Preferred: Prior teaching experience in undergraduate Philosophy (or closely related field) and PPE courses. Application Instructions: Interested and qualified candidates should submit: Cover Letter CV Research Statement Teaching Statement Writing Sample 3 letters of Recommendation For inquires please email: Samantha Aronson saronson@chapman.edu Successful completion of background check is required for final candidate. The salary range for this position is $80,000 - $100,000 a year.  Salary is competitive and will be commensurate with experience. This position is contingent on final budget approval.",
      "department": "The Smith Institute for Political Economy and Philosophy"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190295": {
      "title": "Instructional Assistant Professor of Chemistry, Non-Tenure Track, August 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01 Location: Orange, CA Chapman University seeks applications for an Instructional Assistant Professor of Chemistry (non-tenure track) in the Schmid College of Science and Technology, starting August 2027. Chapman University is a nationally ranked institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. The University achieved R2 status in the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education, a distinction held by just 10 percent of all U.S. universities and is ranked as #110 among Best National Universities by US News and World Report (2026). Our faculty include academic leaders who excel in research, publishing and world-class teaching in our 11 schools and colleges, including the Schmid College of Science and Technology. Dedicated to forward-looking, personalized education, we create an environment for unlimited achievement by both our students and faculty. More information about Chapman University is available at www.chapman.edu . The Schmid College of Science and Technology (SCST) embodies Chapman’s mission of providing students with personalized educational experiences by fostering an outstanding community of teacher-scholars across a broad range of undergraduate and graduate programs. Schmid College has a new 140,000 sq ft home, the Keck Center for Science and Engineering, alongside the newly founded Dale E. and Sarah Ann Fowler School of Engineering. More information about the Schmid College of Science and Technology is available at http://www.chapman.edu/science The undergraduate-only serving Chemistry and Biochemistry Program is home to faculty with specializations in a broad range of fields, including physical, organic, analytical, inorganic, and biochemistry. The ACS-accredited B.S. Chemistry program and the ASBMB-accredited B.S. Biochemistry program are characterized by personalized education, small class sizes, innovative teaching, strong research mentoring, and valuable student-faculty interactions. Instructional faculty play a critical role in the program for teaching, pedagogy, advising, and service. In addition, instructional faculty have the opportunity for promotion to associate and full professor, are senate-eligible, and have full votes at both the program and college levels. Responsibilities The successful candidate will teach four chemistry courses per semester within the Schmid College of Science and Technology, including courses for chemistry majors and chemistry courses for other majors across the campus. This is a full-time, non-tenure track position that requires work to be performed in the State of California and has in-person teaching responsibilities and required on-campus availability at Chapman’s Orange campus. Responsibilities include: Teach Organic Chemistry lecture and laboratory courses. Teach upper-division coursework, e.g. Medicinal Chemistry (BCHM 350), Advanced Organic Chemistry (CHEM 432). Current course descriptions are available at https://catalog.chapman.edu Actively contribute to program activities and engage with the vibrant community of chemistry and biochemistry faculty and students. Actively contribute as an integral member of Schmid College of Science and Technology to the ongoing development, implementation, and assessment of our academic and degree programs. Perform service to the unit, college, university, profession, and/or wider community as expected of an instructional faculty member. A Ph.D. in Chemistry, Chemistry Education, or a related field conferred by the start date. Evidence of disciplinary expertise in organic chemistry and/or medicinal chemistry, including experience teaching these courses. Evidence of effective teaching in higher education at the undergraduate level in STEM courses, including the use of evidence-based teaching practices. Qualified candidates should submit the following electronically via Interfolio: Cover letter detailing qualifications related to the position description. Curriculum Vitae. Statement of teaching philosophy that includes a brief description of past teaching experience in no more than three pages. Two to three items demonstrating evidence of teaching effectiveness. These may include (but are not limited to) student evaluations of teaching, peer/mentor observations of teaching, sample syllabi, or instructional materials. Contact information for three professional references. Letters will be requested for short-listed candidates. Inquiries may be directed to Dr. Matthew Gartner, Search Chair, at schmidcollege@chapman.edu. Please use “Instructional Assistant Professor of Chemistry” as the email subject line. Application review will start on October 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate. This position is subject to final budget approval. Benefits begin the month after the start of employment. The salary range for this Instructional Assistant Professor position is $78,000-$84,000.",
      "department": "Chemistry and Biochemistry"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191338": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer Pool, Fowler School of Engineering, Academic Year 2026-2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10 Location: Orange, CA The Fowler School of Engineering at Chapman University invites applications for its Part-Time Lecturer pool for the Academic year 2026-2027 in the following areas: Computer Science Computer Engineering Electrical Engineering Data Science Software Engineering Appointments from the part-time pool are temporary and often made just prior to the start of the academic term, so applicants must be available on short notice. Fall contracts begin in mid-August and end late-December; Spring contracts begin in mid-January and end mid-May.  Opportunities for teaching during the Summer and Interterm Sessions may also be available. Chapman University is a nationally ranked, R2 Carnegie Classified, private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing 50 states, three territories, and 87 countries. The 11 schools and colleges of Chapman, including Fowler School of Engineering, offer 65 bachelors, 53 master, and six doctoral programs, along with nearly 60 minors and 17 accelerated and bridge programs. Classes are taught by the more than 1,100 dedicated faculty members who are leaders in their fields, including Nobel Prize recipients, MacArthur Fellows, National Medal of Science honorees, former U.S. Supreme Court clerks, and Emmy, Grammy, and Academy Award winners. Opened in the fall of 2019, the Dale E. and Sarah Ann Fowler School of Engineering offers undergraduate degree programs in Computer Science, Computer Engineering, Data Science, Electrical Engineering, and Software Engineering, and a graduate program in Electrical Engineering and Computer Science. The School also offers several interdisciplinary minors and themed inquiry programs, which allow students from across other Chapman programs to explore engineering. Fowler Engineering is committed to contributing solutions to global challenges and a key component of that mission is the recruitment and support of faculty, staff, and students from the broadest possible set of backgrounds and experiences. The Swenson Family Hall of Engineering, which opened in 2021, provides state-of-the-art facilities for teaching and research, enabling truly boundaryless experiences. Responsibilities Fowler School of Engineering seeks talented educators who are committed to student-centered, hands-on learning in a small-class environment. Teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses in your area of expertise Prepare and deliver lectures, assignments, and assessments Hold office hours to support student learning and success Collaborate with colleagues to ensure course outcomes align with program objectives Potential Courses Include: Programming (e.g., Python, C/C++, Java) Data Structures and Algorithms Machine Learning and Data Science Circuits and Electronics Digital Logic and Computer Architecture Artificial Intelligence Software Design Power Systems Required: Master’s degree or PhD in Computer Science, Computer Engineering, Electrical Engineering, Software Engineering, or a closely related field. In lieu of an advanced degree, industry or experiential experience may be considered. Preferred: Prior university-level teaching experience Preferred: Significant industry experience in relevant areas Interested and qualified candidates are invited to electronically submit: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Brief teaching statement (1–2 pages) describing your teaching philosophy and relevant experience List of courses you are qualified to teach Contact information for three references For any inquiries, please contact engineering@chapman.edu. Please use Part-Time Lecturer Pool Inquiry as the subject line. The Fowler School of Engineering will draw from the pool as necessary when openings arise. Only those selected for an interview will be contacted. Applications will remain in the applicant pool until the end of Chapman University’s fiscal year, August 15. Should you remain interested in future openings, you will need to reapply. The pay range for course and course related work for this position is $51.28 per hour. This position is contingent on final budget approval. Successful completion of a background check is required for any and all final candidates selected from the pool.",
      "department": "Fowler School of Engineering"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191122": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer Pool, Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences, 2026-2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02 Deadline: 2027-08-15 Location: Orange Campus Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at Chapman University seeks applications for its Part-Time Lecturer Pool for potential teaching opportunities in the following areas/disciplines: Art (Art, Art History, & Design) Creative & Cultural Industries English (Creative Writing, Literature & Cultural Studies, Journalism, Rhetoric & Composition) History Peace & Justice Studies Political Science Sociology Anthropology World Languages & Cultures (Arabic, Chinese, French, German, Italian, Japanese, and Spanish) Interdisciplinary Minors (Africana Studies, Asian American Studies, Disability Studies, Environmental Studies, Ethnic Studies, Health Humanities, Latinx and Latin American Studies, Law & Liberal Arts, LGBTQ Studies, Religious Studies, and Women’s & Gender Studies) Appointments from the part-time pool are temporary and often made just prior to the start of the academic term, so applicants must be available on very short notice. The Fall term begins mid-August and ends late-December; the Spring term begins mid-January and ends mid-May.  Opportunities for teaching during the Summer and Interterm Sessions may also be available. Chapman University is a nationally ranked institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. The University achieved R2 status in the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education, a distinction held by just 10 percent of all U.S. universities and is ranked as #121 among Best National Universities by US News and World Report (2026). Our faculty include academic leaders who excel in research, publishing, and world-class teaching in our 11 schools and colleges, including Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences . Dedicated to forward-looking, personalized education, we create an environment for unlimited achievement by both our students and faculty. More information about Chapman University is available at www.chapman.edu . Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at Chapman University is a dynamic and interdisciplinary academic community dedicated to fostering critical thinking, creativity, and global citizenship. Home to a diverse range of programs in the arts, humanities, and social sciences, Wilkinson College offers faculty and students opportunities for meaningful research, experiential learning, and collaboration across disciplines. With a commitment to excellence, innovation, and civic engagement, the college provides a supportive environment where part-time lecturers can contribute to shaping transformative educational experiences for students. More information about Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences is available at https://www.chapman.edu/wilkinson/index.aspx . Required Earned graduate degree from an accredited institution or professional equivalency Demonstrated currency in subject matter Strong commitment to student learning, quality teaching, and academic excellence. Ability to motivate and teach students of diverse learning styles and backgrounds. Ability to teach in-person on campus Preferred Earned Graduate degree from an accredited institution in the subject area related to the position being sought Prior teaching experience Qualified candidates are invited to electronically submit the following through Interfolio: Cover letter detailing qualifications related to the position description Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy Statement (limit 2 pages) Names of three references with contact information Additional material may be requested All inquiries should be directed to the relevant disciplinary contact person listed, with \"Part-Time Lecturer Pool\" specified in the subject line. Anthropology - Aimee DeMier, Email: ademier@chapman.edu Art (Art, Art History, and Design) - Ethan Javonillo, Email: javonillo@chapman.edu Creative & Cultural Industries - Shannon Halverson, Email Shalvers@chapman.edu English (Creative Writing, Literature & Cultural Studies, Journalism, Rhetoric & Composition) - Samantha De La O, Email: sadelao@chapman.edu, and Lucy Rust, Email: lrust@chapman.edu History - Sweet Lou Mader Dauk, Email: smaderdauk@chapman.edu Interdisciplinary Minors (Africana Studies, Asian American Studies, Creative and Cultural Industries, Disability Studies, Environmental Studies, Ethnic Studies, Health Humanities, Latinx and Latin American Studies, Law & Liberal Arts, LGBTQ Studies, Religious Studies, and Women & Gender Studies) - Julia Ainley, Email: ainley@chapman.edu Peace & Justice Studies - Julia Ainley, Email: ainley@chapman.edu Political Science - Christian Grevin, Email: cgrevin@chapman.edu Sociology - Aimee DeMier, Email: ademier@chapman.edu World Languages & Cultures (Arabic, Chinese, French, German, Italian, Japanese, and Spanish) - Bonnie Ogles, Email: ogles@chapman.edu Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences will draw from the pool as necessary when openings arise. Only those selected for an interview will be contacted. Applications will remain in the applicant pool until August 15th, 2026. Should you remain interested in future openings, you will need to reapply. Please note: A maximum of 6 credits can be taught per semester. The pay for the course and course-related work for this position for 2026 is $46.85 per hour.  Pay for 2027 will be announced in January 2027.  This position is contingent on final budget approval.  Successful completion of a background check is required for all selected candidates. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States.",
      "department": "Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190860": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Communication Sciences and Disorders, Tenure Track, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-07 Location: Irvine, Rinker Campus Crean College of Health and Behavioral Sciences at Chapman University invites applications for a Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in the Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders, Fall 2027.  Area of expertise is open, with preference given to candidates with a strong research background and potential in acquired or developmental language disorders. Chapman University is a nationally-ranked, R2 Carnegie Classified, private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions.  Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing 50 states, three territories, and 87 countries.  The 11 schools and colleges of Chapman, including Crean College of Health and Behavioral Sciences, offer 65 bachelors, 53 master, and six doctoral programs, along with nearly 60 minors and 17 accelerated and bridge programs.  Classes are taught by the more than 1,100 dedicated faculty members who are leaders in their fields, including Nobel Prize recipients, MacArthur Fellows, National Medal of Science honorees, former U.S. Supreme Court clerks, and Emmy, Grammy, and Academy Award winners. The Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders (CSD) is located on the Rinker Health Science campus in Irvine, CA.  This campus is designed to foster an inter-professional educational environment and to use cutting edge technology in teaching and practice.  The campus features state of the art facilities, including a human cadaver anatomy laboratory, advanced learning center with innovative technology, Grand Rounds auditorium with 3D projection capabilities, multi-sized classrooms, a medical library, and collaborative work centers.  The campus is home to several accredited graduate health science professional programs, including Doctor of Physical Therapy, Master of Science in Communication Sciences and Disorders, Master of Science Physician Assistant Studies, and Doctor of Pharmacy.  The Rinker campus is strategically located near healthcare institutions, medical device manufacturers, and biopharmaceutical companies. The Communication Sciences and Disorders (CSD) department is seeking a colleague who is dynamic and collaborative; a strong investigator who will provide our students with state-of-the-art academic instruction and help us build our research profile.  As a Master of Science graduate degree program, the CSD department is accredited by the Council of Academic Accreditation (CAA) and the Commission on Teacher Credentialing (CTC).  The CSD department is associated with numerous school districts, private practices, and medical centers that provide services to individuals with communication disorders across the lifespan.  The diversity of Southern California is reflected in the CSD student cohorts, where 55% of students self-identify as members of racial or ethnic minority communities.  For more information about the Communication Sciences and Disorders department, please visit the CSD website . Responsibilities Conduct and disseminate research in their area of expertise. Seek and obtain extramural funding to support research. Teach primarily in academic area of expertise and contribute to teaching Capstone course, for which students complete a research project in their graduating year. Be involved in faculty governance. Provide regular service expected from a faculty member. Tenure-track faculty are expected to develop and maintain an active research agenda for future tenure consideration and are evaluated on their research/scholarly activity, teaching, and service. Required Doctorate degree in Communication Sciences and Disorders or a broadly related field. Clinical Certification as a Speech-Language Pathologist, by the American Speech-Language-Hearing Association (ASHA). Strong communication, organizational, interpersonal, problem-solving, and mentoring skills. Strong research portfolio at a junior level, on an upward trajectory. Able to work independently and coordinate work with colleagues and peers. Current knowledge and skill in the use of information and computer technology (e.g., word processing, e-mail, database, web-based applications) or proven ability to learn these skills. Experience supporting students with disabilities and diverse learning needs in accordance with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Effective oral and written communication skills. Desired Prior college-level teaching experience in the field of communication sciences and disorders. Licensed or eligible for licensure in the state of California. Interested and qualified applicants should submit: Letter of application. Curriculum Vitae. Statements of teaching and research philosophy. Up to three peer-reviewed publications or reprints in pdf format that best align with the statement of research. If available, up to three student course evaluations or other evidence of effectiveness as a college-level instructor. Three names of referees that can be contacted for confidential letters of recommendation. For inquiries about this position, please contact Dirk den Ouden, PhD, Search Committee Chair, at denouden@chapman.edu . The position will begin in Fall 2027.  Salary and start-up packages are competitive and will be based on experience, rank, and funding status.  Teaching load is consistent with R2 status and will accommodate 50% time devoted to research activities. We offer competitive salaries and excellent benefits that include a substantial contribution toward the cost of medical, vision, and dental insurance, retirement and pension plans, and flexible work arrangements to support work/life balance for our employees. The position is open until filled, but preference will be given to applications submitted before October 15 th , 2026 . Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate.  The salary range for this position is $100,000 - $156,000.  Salary is competitive and will be commensurate with experience.  This position is contingent on final budget approval.",
      "department": "Communication Sciences and Disorders"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190862": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor of Communication Sciences and Disorders, Non-Tenure-Track, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-07 Location: Irvine, Rinker Campus Crean College of Health and Behavioral Sciences at Chapman University invites applications for a full-time Clinical Assistant Professor with expertise in child language development, autism, and/or school-age language and literacy , for its Master of Science program in Communication Sciences and Disorders, at the rank of Non-Tenure-Track Assistant Professor, beginning Fall 2027. Chapman University is a nationally-ranked, R2 Carnegie Classified, private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing 50 states, three territories, and 87 countries. The 11 schools and colleges of Chapman, including Crean College of Health and Behavioral Sciences, offer 65 bachelors, 53 master, and six doctoral programs, along with nearly 60 minors and 17 accelerated and bridge programs. Classes are taught by the more than 1,100 dedicated faculty members who are leaders in their fields, including Nobel Prize recipients, MacArthur Fellows, National Medal of Science honorees, former U.S. Supreme Court clerks, and Emmy, Grammy, and Academy Award winners. The Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders (CSD) is located on the Rinker Health Science campus in Irvine, CA. This campus is designed to foster an inter-professional educational environment and to use cutting edge technology in teaching and practice.  The campus features state of the art facilities, including a human cadaver anatomy laboratory, advanced learning center with innovative technology, Grand Rounds auditorium with 3D projection capabilities, multi-sized classrooms, a medical library, and collaborative work centers. The campus is home to several accredited graduate health science professional programs, including Doctor of Physical Therapy, Master of Science in Communication Sciences and Disorders, Master of Science Physician Assistant Studies, and Doctor of Pharmacy. The Rinker campus is strategically located near healthcare institutions, medical device manufacturers, and biopharmaceutical companies. The CSD department is seeking a colleague who is experienced, dynamic, collaborative, and an effective instructor; one who will provide our students with state-of-the-art academic and practice-based clinical knowledge, and build upon the currently existing network in the community, schools, private practices, and hospital settings. As a Master of Science graduate degree program, the CSD department is accredited by the Council of Academic Accreditation (CAA) and the Commission on Teacher Credentialing (CTC). The CSD department is associated with numerous school districts, private practices, and medical centers that provide services to individuals with communication disorders across the lifespan. The diversity of Southern California is reflected in the CSD student cohorts, where 55% of students self-identify as members of racial or ethnic minority communities.  For more information about the Communication Sciences and Disorders department, please visit the visit the CSD website . Responsibilities Teach courses in child language development, autism, school-age language and literacy and/or clinical-practicum. Depending on expertise and interest, there is an opportunity to teach Capstone course, for which students complete a research project in their graduating year. Provide regular service expected from a full-time faculty member. Required Master of Sciences or Master of Arts graduate degree in Speech, Language, and Hearing Science (or the equivalent in Speech-Language Pathology/Communication Sciences and Disorders) with demonstrated excellence in clinical education. Graduate of an accredited communication sciences and disorders professional program. Clinical Certification as a Speech-Language Pathologist, by the American Speech-Language-Hearing Association (ASHA). Licensed or eligible for licensure in the state of California. Minimum of 2 years of clinical teaching, clinical coordination and/or clinical supervision. Strong communication, organizational, interpersonal, problem-solving, and clinical mentoring skills. Able to work independently and coordinate work with colleagues and peers, especially with colleagues involved with clinical education. Current knowledge and skill in the use of information and computer technology (e.g., word processing, e-mail, database, web-based applications) or proven ability to learn these skills. Able to work with students with special needs based on the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Effective oral and written communication skills. Desired Minimum of 5 years’ experience as a certified Speech-Language Pathologist. Prior clinical teaching experiences in a CSD academic program and/or administrative experience in clinical practice. Multicultural clinical experience and ability to teach cultural awareness. Doctorate degree in Communication Sciences and Disorders or a broadly related field. Interested and qualified applicants should submit: Letter of application. Curriculum Vitae. Statement of teaching philosophy. If available, up to three student course evaluations or other evidence of effectiveness as a college-level instructor. Three confidential letters of recommendation. For inquiries about this position, please contact Dirk den Ouden, PhD, Search Committee Chair, at denouden@chapman.edu . We offer competitive salaries and excellent benefits that include a substantial contribution toward the cost of medical, vision, and dental insurance, retirement and pension plans, and flexible work arrangements to support work/life balance for our employees. The position is open until filled, but preference will be given to applications submitted before October 15 th , 2026 . Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate. The salary range for this position is $100,000 - $116,000. Salary is competitive and will be commensurate with experience.  This position is contingent on final budget approval.",
      "department": "Communication Sciences and Disorders"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190695": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Environmental Science and Policy and Program Director, Tenured, August 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09 Deadline: 2026-10-11 Location: Orange, CA Chapman University invites applications for an Associate or Full Professor of Environmental Science & Policy and Program Director in the Schmid College of Science and Technology starting August 2027. This is a full-time, tenured position that requires work to be performed in the State of California with specific in-person teaching responsibilities and required on-campus availability at Chapman’s Orange campus. Chapman University is a nationally ranked R2 institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Our faculty are academic leaders who excel in both research and teaching. Dedicated to forward-looking, personalized education, we create an environment for unlimited achievement by both our students and faculty. More information about Chapman University is available at www.chapman.edu. The Environmental Science & Policy Program is uniquely seated within two colleges at Chapman University: the Schmid College of Science and Technology and the Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences. Schmid and Wilkinson Colleges embody Chapman’s mission of providing students with personalized educational experiences by fostering an outstanding community of teacher-scholars across a broad range of undergraduate and graduate programs. Schmid College has a new 140,000 sq ft home, the Keck Center for Science and Engineering. More information about Schmid and Wilkinson Colleges is available at www.chapman.edu/science and www.chapman.edu/wilkinson , respectively. The undergraduate-only serving Environmental Science & Policy Program is home to a diverse group of faculty with interdisciplinary specializations across the breadth of environmental sciences (ecology, engineering, hydrology, remote sensing) and environmental studies (anthropology, environmental justice, natural resource governance, political science, sociology). The program serves the interdisciplinary Environmental Science & Policy B.S. degree, the Environmental Science minor, and the Environmental Studies minor. We seek candidates with an active research program in areas including but not limited to: Environmental health Environmental toxicology Water, soil, and/or air quality Environmental contaminants Natural resource governance We value candidates who can identify avenues for collaboration both within and outside of the institution. Faculty Role Responsibilities The successful candidate will teach lower- and upper-level courses that contribute to the major and minors within the Environmental Science & Policy Program, maintain an active research program, and advise/mentor undergraduate research students. Significant scholarship, including publishing in peer-reviewed journals, presenting at relevant research conferences, and obtaining and managing extramural grants is an expectation of the position. Required responsibilities include: Teach lower- and upper-division Environmental Science & Policy courses, including core and elective courses. Current course descriptions are available at https://catalog.chapman.edu . Continue a research program in interdisciplinary environmental sciences, including publishing papers and presenting work in this area at local and national conferences. Actively contribute as an integral member of the Schmid College of Science and Technology in the ongoing development, implementation, and assessment of their academic and degree programs. Perform service to the unit, college, university, profession, and wider community as expected of a tenured faculty member. Program Director Responsibilities The successful candidate will lead the Environmental Science & Policy faculty and degree program as Program Director for a minimum of three years in an at-will, compensated appointment separate from their faculty role. Required responsibilities include: Provide leadership, vision, and a voice for affiliated faculty, students, and degree programs. Oversee program budget and advocate on behalf of the program for resource use. Lead curriculum alignment and revisions, assessment reports, and course scheduling for the program. Coordinate and participate in program and college events, including events for student recruitment and commencement, as appropriate. Assign faculty to courses and hire appropriate part-time lecturers where needed. Facilitate communication and collaboration between Schmid and Wilkinson Colleges pertaining to the support of this interdisciplinary program. An earned Ph.D. in one of the environmental sciences (e.g., Biology, Chemistry, Environmental Science & Management) or related field by the start of employment from an accredited institution. Earned tenure at the current institution of employment or equivalent. A strong history of interdisciplinary environmental science research, including evidence of an independent, nationally recognized, externally funded research program. Evidence of effective teaching at the undergraduate level, particularly in experiential learning courses, such as a senior capstone course, or upper division coursework. Experience in curriculum development and learning outcome assessment. Experience in leadership or administrative roles in academic service settings, such as college governance, curriculum, or assessment committees, ideally connected to interdisciplinary endeavors . Qualified candidates should submit the following electronically via Interfolio: Cover letter detailing qualifications related to the position description. Curriculum Vitae. Statement of teaching philosophy that includes a brief description of past teaching experience, no more than two pages. Research Statement that details current and future environmental science research, no more than two pages. Statement of leadership philosophy that includes a brief description of administrative and leadership experiences as they relate to Program Director-level service, no more than one page. Contact information for three professional references. Letters will be requested from short-listed candidates. Inquiries may be directed to Richelle Tanner, Search Committee Chair, at schmidcollege@chapman.edu. Please use “Environmental Science & Policy Faculty Position” as the email subject line. Application review will begin October 11, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled.  This position is subject to final budget approval. Salary range for this position is $105,000 - $125,000. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate.",
      "department": "Environmental Science & Policy"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191035": {
      "title": "Writing Center Director and Associate or Full Professor of English, Tenured, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02 Location: Orange, CA The Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at Chapman University invites applications for a Writing Center Director and Associate or Full Professor of English, beginning 1 August 2027. Chapman University is a nationally-ranked, R2 Carnegie Classified, private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing 50 states, 4 territories, and 76 countries. The 11 schools and colleges of Chapman, including Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences, offer 50 bachelors, 29 masters, and 8 doctoral programs, along with nearly 75 minors and 19 accelerated and bridge programs. Classes are taught by the more than 1,100 dedicated faculty members who are leaders in their fields, including Nobel Prize recipients, National Medal of Science honorees, former U.S. Supreme Court clerks, and Emmy, Grammy, and Academy Award winners. The mission of Wilkinson College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences is to foster rigorous inquiry among our students through a curriculum grounded in the liberal arts that is scholarly, creative, interdisciplinary, and experiential. Wilkinson College plays an important and unique role in the educational journey of every Chapman student and aims to ensure that graduates are prepared to assume their responsibility as global citizens, acting knowledgeably and providing compassionate leadership in our community and world. More information about Wilkinson College is available at http://www.chapman.edu/wilkinson . The Department of English offers undergraduate majors that integrate journalism, creative writing, cultural studies, literature, and rhetoric, and MA and MFA degree programs at the graduate level. Tenured faculty members are expected to maintain an active research and publication agenda, and are evaluated on their scholarship, teaching, administration, and service. More information about the Department of English is available at https://www.chapman.edu/wilkinson/english/ . We seek a visionary Writing Center Director to lead and foster a Writing Center of distinction that recognizes the critical cognitive importance of writing in the education of our students. The successful candidate will hold a tenured faculty appointment in the Department of English and receive a separate administrative appointment as the Writing Center Director.  We are especially interested in candidates who have scholarly expertise in writing center studies and candidates committed to the importance of literacy and writing in the age of AI. Chapman's Writing Center currently serves undergraduate and graduate students through in-person walk-in appointments, a course consultancy program, synchronous Zoom appointments, asynchronous document review, specialized graduate services for longer-term projects, and in-class workshops. Faculty Responsibilities: Regularly teach undergraduate and graduate courses in the candidate’s areas of expertise Maintain a rigorous research and publication program Advise and mentor students Actively participate in departmental, college, and university service Create an inclusive and welcoming learning environment Writing Center Director Responsibilities: Lead and expand the Writing Center’s reach at Chapman University Develop a strategic plan focused on writing and learning in the age of AI Oversee Writing Center staff and budget Required Qualifications: At least two years’ experience as director of a writing center or equivalent Earned PhD from an accredited institution in English, Rhetoric and Composition, or related field A record of distinguished scholarship, teaching, service, and/or administration commensurate with a tenured appointment at the rank of associate or full professor Preferred Qualifications: Publications in the field of writing center scholarship Qualified candidates are invited to electronically submit Letter of application Curriculum Vitae Names and contact information of three referees Shortlisted candidate will be asked to submit the following additional materials: three confidential letters of recommendation, writing sample, statement of candidate’s vision for the writing center at Chapman University (750 words maximum), student evaluations of teaching. Direct inquiries to the Search Committee Chair, Professor Ian Barnard (barnard@chapman.edu) and use “Writing Center Director” as the email subject line. Application review will begin 30 October 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. The salary range for this position is $110k - $160k. Salary is competitive and will be commensurate with experience. This position is contingent on final budget approval. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate.",
      "department": "English"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191512": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow at the Institute for the Study of Religion, Economics and Society (IRES) - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15 Location: Orange, CA The Institute for the Study of Religion, Economics, and Society (IRES) at Chapman University is currently accepting applications for a postdoctoral fellowship in the economics of religion. The fellowship will commence in Fall 2027 and may be renewed for up to two years. Chapman University is a nationally ranked, R2 Carnegie-classified private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing all 50 states, 4 territories, and 76 countries. The 11 schools and colleges of Chapman offer 50 bachelor's, 29 master's, and 8 doctoral programs, along with nearly 75 minors and 19 accelerated and bridge programs. Classes are taught by more than 1,100 dedicated faculty members who are leaders in their fields, including Nobel Prize recipients, MacArthur Fellows, National Medal of Science honorees, former U.S. Supreme Court clerks, and Emmy, Grammy, and Academy Award winners. The Institute for the Study of Religion, Economics, and Society (IRES) was founded at Chapman University in September 2009 by Dr. Laurence Iannaccone. IRES is unique in its aim to enhance our comprehension of the connections between religion, economy, and society through an interdisciplinary approach. The institute brings together economists, sociologists, and other social scientists in the study of religion. This is accomplished through various means, such as scholarly publications, research seminars, training workshops, conferences, courses, online resources, and public lectures. IRES is home to the Association for the Study of Religion, Economics, and Culture (ASREC) and partners with the Association of Religion Data Archives (ARDA), positioning the institute as a hub within a rapidly expanding international network of scholars and students. Responsibilities This fellowship offers an exceptional opportunity for highly promising scholars to conduct research at Chapman University's Institute for the Study of Religion, Economics, and Society (IRES). We seek scholars who can connect the study of religion to economics, political science, or sociology. Selected candidates will conduct research in one or more of these disciplines while at Chapman. They will also be expected to attend seminars and workshops, present their work at least once during the fellowship, and contribute to other IRES activities. Applicants must have received a Ph.D. in Economics, Sociology, Political Science, or a related discipline between August 1, 2023, and August 1, 2027. Applicants should also demonstrate: A strong record of academic achievement, including publications, working papers, awards, grants, or other scholarly accomplishments. A high-quality research agenda and proposed project demonstrating originality, rigor, feasibility, and potential contribution to the field. A research paper that is published, accepted for publication, or available as a working paper. The ability to present scholarly research to academic audiences. An interest in conducting research at the Institute for the Study of Religion, Economics, and Society (IRES) and engaging meaningfully with the Institute's research community. Qualified candidates are invited to submit the following: 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. Research Paper 4. Letter of Recommendation The deadline for receipt of all materials is January 24, 2027. Applications are accepted only via the link in this listing. Inquiries and questions can be directed to ires@chapman.edu . Please use \" Postdoctoral Fellow (IRES) - Fall 2027\" , as the email subject line. Application reviews will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. The salary is $72,384. This position is contingent on final budget approval. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate.",
      "department": "The Institute for the Study of Religion, Economics and Society"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191038": {
      "title": "Artistic Assistant Professor of Screenwriting, Non-Tenure Track, Spring 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04 Location: Orange Campus Chapman University’s Dodge College of Film and Media Arts seeks a seasoned screenwriter to join our faculty as an Artistic Assistant Professor of Screenwriting, Non-Tenure Track, effective Spring 2027. The ideal candidate will bring professional experience, teaching skill, and a passion for nurturing the next generation of screenwriters. Chapman University is a nationally ranked institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. The University achieved R2 status in the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education, a distinction held by just 10 percent of all U.S. universities, and is ranked #110 among Best National Universities by US News and World Report (2026). Our faculty includes academic leaders who excel in research, publishing, and world-class teaching in our 11 schools and colleges, including Dodge College of Film and Media Arts. Dedicated to forward-looking, personalized education, we create an environment for unlimited student and faculty achievement. Industry trade publications The Wrap and The Hollywood Reporter ranks Dodge College #4 among film schools nationwide. More information about the university and Dodge College is available at https://www.chapman.edu/dodge/index.aspx . Chapman University is dedicated to forward-looking, personalized education and creating a student-centered experience. We strive to create an environment that fosters unlimited achievement by our students and faculty. Responsibilities: TEACHING AND MENTORING .  Eight courses per academic year (for example: four in the fall + four in the spring).  Courses such as introductory writing for film and television for freshmen; intermediate writing classes in television, feature film, and/or new and emerging media for sophomores and juniors; advanced thesis screenwriting courses for seniors; and similar courses for graduate students. Advising and mentoring BFA and MFA students as they progress through their programs are also required. SERVICE. Participate actively on university and Dodge College committees and other service projects as assigned. CREATIVE ACTIVITY. Maintain currency in the industry through continued screenwriting and/or creative professional work. Required: Significant experience working as a screenwriter in film, television, emerging media or related formats, producing work that has achieved industry recognition Broad knowledge of screenwriting and film industry practices, especially as it relates to low-budget, student filmmaking and writing producible content Expertise in teaching the art of storytelling across a wide variety of formats An ability to inspire students artistically, to help them think critically, to enable them to collaborate as team members, and to guide them finding their voices as storytellers Desired: Membership in professionally recognized industry organizations, such as the Writers Guild of America, Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences, and/or Academy of Television Arts and Sciences Previous teaching experience A master’s degree or significant equivalent professional experience Experience with new and emerging formats, such as verticals and webisodes Interested and qualified candidates should submit: CV or resume Cover letter Link to portfolio or reel of professional work Letters of recommendation will be requested as part of the selection process and need not be submitted with the initial application. Inquiries can be directed to search committee chair, Joe Slowensky, ftvsearch@chapman.edu . Please use \"FT Screenwriting Faculty Position\" as the email subject line. Application reviews will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate. The salary range for this position is $100,000-$110,000. Salary is competitive and will be commensurate with experience. This postition is contingent on final budget approval.",
      "department": "Dodge College of Film and Media Arts"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191968": {
      "title": "Part-time Lecturer Pool, Schmid College of Science and Technology, 2026-2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21 Deadline: 2027-08-15 Location: Orange, CA Schmid College of Science and Technology at Chapman University seeks applications for its Part-Time Lecturer Pool for potential teaching opportunities in the following disciplines: Biological Sciences Biochemistry Chemistry Environmental Science & Policy Food Science Mathematics Philosophy Physics Appointments from the part-time pool are temporary and often made just prior to the start of the academic term, so applicants must be available at times on very short notice. Fall contracts begin in mid-August and end late-December; Spring contracts begin in mid-January and end mid-May. Opportunities for teaching during the Summer and Interterm Sessions may also be available. Chapman University is a nationally-ranked institution offering traditional undergraduate and  graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. The University achieved R2 status in the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education, a distinction held by just 10 percent of all U.S. universities and is ranked as #110 among Best National Universities by US News and World Report (2026). Our faculty include academic leaders who excel in research, publishing, and world-class teaching in our 11 schools and colleges, including the Schmid College of Science and Technology. Dedicated to forward-looking, personalized education, we create an environment for unlimited achievement by both our students and faculty. More information about Chapman University is available at www.chapman.edu . Schmid College of Science and Technology (SCST) embodies Chapman’s mission of providing students with personalized educational experiences by fostering an outstanding community of teacher-scholars across a broad range of undergraduate and graduate programs. Schmid College has a new 140,000 sq ft home, the Keck Center for Science and Engineering, alongside the newly founded Dale E. and Sarah Ann Fowler School of Engineering. More information about the Schmid College of Science and Technology is available at http://www.chapman.edu/science . Key Responsibilities: Teach one or two lab or lecture courses during the term. Maintain regular office hours, attend instructor meetings and orientations as required. Required qualifications: Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution in the subject area being taught. Ability to communicate clearly and effectively orally and in writing. Strong commitment to student learning, quality teaching, and student excellence. Ability to teach in-person on our campus in Orange, CA. Preferred qualifications: Graduate level degree from an accredited institution in the subject area being taught. Demonstrated experience in teaching at the undergraduate level, or relevant field experience. Interested candidates should submit the following materials via Interfolio: 1.      Cover letter 2.      Current CV 3.      Teaching Philosophy Statement 4.      Contact information for three professional references Please address any questions to Rebecca Green at schmidcollege@chapman.edu , using \"Part-Time Lecturer Pool Inquiry\" as the email subject line. Schmid College of Science and Technology will draw from the pool as necessary when openings arise. Only those selected for an interview will be contacted. Applications will remain in the pool until August 15, 2027. Should you remain interested in future openings beyond that date, you will need to re-apply to the next academic year’s posting. The pay range for course and course related work for this position is $48.24 per hour. This position is contingent on final budget approval.  Successful completion of a background check is required for all selected candidates.",
      "department": "Schmid College of Science and Technology"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190902": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Philosophy, Tenure-Track, August 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21 Location: Orange, CA Chapman University invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor in Philosophy, beginning August 1, 2027. The appointment will be housed in the Philosophy Department within Schmid College of Science and Technology. The successful candidate will also have an affiliation with the Smith Institute for Political Economy and Philosophy and contribute to its teaching. Successful candidates are expected to conduct independent and original research at the highest academic level, provide excellent teaching, and demonstrate academic leadership. Since the candidate will be jointly affiliated with the Smith Institute, we seek candidates interested in working together with people from different disciplinary backgrounds. In particular, candidates should demonstrate an interest in putting their lines of philosophical inquiry into conversation with insights from economics or the other social sciences and humanities. Chapman University is a nationally-ranked institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. The University achieved R2 status in the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education, a distinction held by just 10 percent of all U.S. universities and is ranked as #110 among Best National Universities by US News and World Report (2026). Our faculty include academic leaders who excel in research, publishing, and world-class teaching in our 11 schools and colleges, including the Schmid College of Science and Technology. Dedicated to forward-looking, personalized education, we create an environment for unlimited achievement by both our students and faculty. More information about Chapman University is available at www.chapman.edu . The mission of the Smith Institute is to reintegrate the study of the humanities and economics in the spirit of Adam Smith, the author of The Theory of Moral Sentiments and An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations , and to recombine research and undergraduate education as a discovery process in the spirit of Vernon Smith, a founder of experimental economics. Tenure-track faculty are expected to develop and maintain an active research agenda for future tenure consideration and are evaluated on their research/scholarly activity, teaching, and service. Required: PhD in Philosophy or its equivalent from an accredited institution. Strong potential for impactful scholarly contributions in the field of Philosophy, or a closely related field. Commitment to teaching and mentoring. A desire to work in an interdisciplinary environment. Preferred: Prior teaching experience in undergraduate Philosophy (or closely related field) and PPE courses. Specialty in one or more of the following: Political Philosophy, PPE (Philosophy, Politics and Economics), and the History of Political and Economic Thought. Interested and qualified candidates should submit the following via Interfolio: Cover Letter CV Research Statement Teaching Statement Writing Sample Three Confidential Letters of Recommendation Inquiries may be directed to Bas van der Vossen, Search Committee Chair, at schmidcollege@chapman.edu. Please use “Philosophy Faculty Position” as the email subject line. Application review will begin November 2, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate. The salary range for the position is $85,000 - $98,000 per year. Position is contingent on final budget approval.",
      "department": "Philosophy"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191214": {
      "title": "Instructional Assistant Professor of Mathematics, Non-Tenure Track, August 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21 Location: Orange, CA Chapman University seeks applications for an Instructional Assistant Professor of Mathematics (non-tenure track) in the Schmid College of Science and Technology, starting August 2027. Chapman University is a nationally ranked institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. The University achieved R2 status in the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education, a distinction held by just 10 percent of all U.S. universities and is ranked as #110 among Best National Universities by US News and World Report (2026). Our faculty include academic leaders who excel in research, publishing and world-class teaching in our 11 schools and colleges, including Schmid College of Science and Technology. Dedicated to forward-looking, personalized education, we create an environment for unlimited achievement by both our students and faculty. More information about Chapman University is available at www.chapman.edu . Schmid College of Science and Technology (SCST) embodies Chapman’s mission of providing students with personalized educational experiences by fostering an outstanding community of teacher-scholars across a broad range of undergraduate and graduate programs. Schmid College has a new 140,000 sq ft home, the Keck Center for Science and Engineering, alongside the newly founded Dale E. and Sarah Ann Fowler School of Engineering. More information about the Schmid College of Science and Technology is available at http://www.chapman.edu/science . The Mathematics Program is home to faculty with specializations in a broad range of fields, including complex and hypercomplex analysis, algebra and topology, statistics and biostatistics, mathematical modeling, and mathematics education. The B.S. in Mathematics program, along with the dual B.S. in Mathematics and Civil Engineering, is characterized by personalized education, small class sizes, innovative teaching, and strong research mentoring and student-faculty interactions. Instructional faculty play critical roles in the program for teaching, pedagogy, advising, and service. In addition, instructional faculty have the opportunity for promotion to associate and full professor, are senate-eligible, and have full votes at both the program and college levels. Responsibilities The successful candidate will teach four math courses per semester within the Schmid College of Science and Technology.  This is a full-time, non-tenure track position that requires work to be performed in the State of California and has in-person teaching responsibilities and required on-campus availability at Chapman’s Orange campus. Responsibilities include: Teach freshman and sophomore level mathematics courses with a primary focus on 100-level courses (e.g., Math 100, Math 108, Math 109) and relevant supplemental courses. Current course descriptions are available at https://catalog.chapman.edu . Actively contribute to program activities and engage with the vibrant community of mathematics faculty and students. Actively contribute as an integral member of Schmid College of Science and Technology in the ongoing development, implementation, and assessment of our academic and degree programs. Perform service to the unit, college, university, profession, and wider community as expected of an instructional faculty member. Ph.D. in Mathematics or a related field conferred by the start date. Evidence of effective teaching in higher education at the undergraduate level, particularly introductory mathematics courses. Experience with implementation of innovative teaching practices, including using technology to enhance educational environments. Strong interpersonal communication skills. Qualified candidates should submit the following electronically via Interfolio: Cover letter detailing qualifications related to the position description Curriculum Vitae Statement of teaching philosophy that includes a brief description of past teaching experience, no more than three pages Contact information for three professional references. Letters will be requested from short-listed candidates. Inquiries may be directed to Dr. Nicol R. Howard, Search Chair, at schmidcollege@chapman.edu. Please use “Instructional Assistant Professor of Math” as the email subject line. Application review will start on October 21, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate. This position is subject to final budget approval. Benefits begin the month after the start of your employment. The salary range for this Instructional Assistant Professor position is $76,000 – $85,000.",
      "department": "Math"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188373": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Computer Science and Electrical Engineering, Fall 2026",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-21 Location: Chapman University Dale E. and Sarah Ann Fowler School of Engineering The rapidly growing Dale E. and Sarah Ann Fowler School of Engineering at Chapman University invites applications for a post-doctoral fellow position in the area of Computer Science and Electrical Engineering for Fall 2026. Chapman University is a nationally ranked, R2 Carnegie Classified, private institution offering traditional undergraduate and graduate programs in the heart of Orange County, one of Southern California’s most diverse and vibrant regions. Chapman’s campuses are home to nearly 10,000 students representing 50 states, three territories, and 87 countries. The 11 schools and colleges of Chapman, including Fowler School of Engineering, offer 65 bachelors, 53 master, and six doctoral programs, along with 60 minors and 17 integrated and bridge programs. Classes are taught by more than 1,100 dedicated faculty members who are leaders in their fields, including Nobel Prize recipients, MacArthur Fellows, National Medal of Science honorees, former U.S. Supreme Court clerks, and Emmy, Grammy, and Academy Award winners. Opened in the fall of 2019, the Dale E. and Sarah Ann Fowler School of Engineering offers undergraduate degree programs in Computer Science, Computer Engineering, Data Science, Electrical Engineering, and Software Engineering, and a graduate program in Electrical Engineering and Computer Science. The School also offers several interdisciplinary minors and themed inquiry programs which allow students from across other Chapman programs to explore engineering. Fowler Engineering is committed to contributing solutions to global challenges and a key component of that mission is the recruitment and support of faculty, staff, and students from the broadest possible set of backgrounds and experiences. The Swenson Family Hall of Engineering, which opened in 2021, provides state-of-the-art facilities for teaching and research that allow for experiences that are truly without boundaries. A postdoctoral position is available for Fall 2026 for highly motivated individuals to join Dr. Etezad and Dr. Cibrian's research group. Dr. Etezad’s research focuses on assistive technology and education,  through the design of tactile and Braille-based interfaces, interactive STEM learning tools, and accessible educational technologies for individuals with visual impairments. Dr. Cibrian works at the intersection of HCI, assistive technology, ubiquitous computing, and AI using user-centered methods (contextual studies, co-design, prototyping, user evaluations). Together, they aim to design, develop and evaluate STEM games paired with an affordable refreshable Braille display for elementary students with blindness and visual impairment. Candidates with a background in one or more of the relevant research areas are encouraged to apply.  Successful candidates will engage in day-to-day research activities aligned with their expertise, contributing to projects within these areas. The position also offers opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration with scholars and researchers across related fields. Responsibilities The postdoctoral researcher will conduct independent research and/or collaborate on multiple research projects; provide guidance and support to undergraduate/graduate students associated with the related projects; document research activities and publish key findings in high-quality, peer-reviewed journals. Required Qualifications A doctoral or equivalent degree in engineering, computer science, statistics, or related fields is required at the time of employment and awarded within the last five years. Excellent verbal and written skills and strong programming skills. Desired Qualifications Experience in one or more of the following areas is preferred: assistive technology, user studies, statistical data analysis, electronics, human–computer interaction, artificial intelligence, or educational technology. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with a proven ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment. Currently authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis. Interested applicants should submit the following documents: 1.  Detailed curriculum vitae 2.  Cover letter 3.  Research statement indicating research interests, summary of previous research portfolio, and future goals 4.  Contact information of three references (reference letters are encouraged but are not required at the time of application) Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled.  Please address any questions to Dr. Etezad: etezadbrojerdi@chapman.edu or Dr. Cibrian: cibrian@chapman.edu The start date of the position is flexible but is anticipated to begin in Fall 2026. The salary range for this Postdoctoral Research Fellow position is $70,304 - $75,000 and is contingent on final budget approval. Salary is competitive and will be commensurate with experience. Successful completion of a background check is required for the final candidate. Chapman University is committed to providing a comprehensive employee benefits program. More information on Chapman’s benefits program may be found on the Office of Human Resources website. Chapman University is an equal opportunity employer that provides equal employment opportunities to all individuals, regardless of their protected characteristics.  All qualified applicants and employees are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, national origin, ancestry, citizenship status, physical disability, mental disability, medical condition, military and veteran status, marital status, pregnancy, genetic information or any other characteristic protected by state or federal law.",
      "department": "Fowler School of Engineering"
    }
  },
